# Graph BFS and DFS: Major LeetCode Patterns

## Core Idea

```text
DFS → Goes deeply into one path before returning
BFS → Explores nodes level by level
```

A graph problem may be hidden as:

```text
Grid cells       → Nodes
Cell movement    → Edges

Courses          → Nodes
Prerequisites    → Directed edges

Words            → Nodes
Transformations  → Edges
```

---

# 1. Basic DFS Traversal

## Use When

- Exploring all reachable nodes
- Marking connected nodes
- Flood fill
- Processing one complete component

```java
private void dfs(
    int node,
    List<List<Integer>> graph,
    boolean[] visited
) {
    visited[node] = true;

    for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
        if (!visited[neighbor]) {
            dfs(neighbor, graph, visited);
        }
    }
}
```

## Important Rule

```text
Mark the node visited before exploring its neighbors.
```

---

# 2. Basic BFS Traversal

## Use When

- Exploring level by level
- Finding minimum steps
- Finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph

```java
private void bfs(
    int start,
    List<List<Integer>> graph,
    boolean[] visited
) {
    Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

    visited[start] = true;
    queue.offer(start);

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int node = queue.poll();

        for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
            if (!visited[neighbor]) {
                visited[neighbor] = true;
                queue.offer(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Important Rule

```text
Mark a node visited when adding it to the queue,
not when removing it.
```

---

# 3. Connected Components

## Recognition

Look for:

- Number of islands
- Number of provinces
- Number of connected groups
- Number of separate networks

## Pattern

```java
int components = 0;
boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

for (int node = 0; node < n; node++) {
    if (!visited[node]) {
        components++;
        dfs(node, graph, visited);
    }
}
```

## Mental Model

```text
Outer loop → Finds a new component
DFS/BFS    → Explores the complete component
```

## Problems

- 200. Number of Islands
- 547. Number of Provinces

---

# 4. Grid DFS

## Recognition

Look for:

- Islands
- Connected cells
- Flood fill
- Area of a region
- Surrounded regions

## Pattern

```java
private void dfs(int row, int col, int[][] grid) {
    if (
        row < 0 ||
        col < 0 ||
        row >= grid.length ||
        col >= grid[0].length ||
        grid[row][col] == 0
    ) {
        return;
    }

    // Mark the cell as visited.
    grid[row][col] = 0;

    dfs(row - 1, col, grid);
    dfs(row + 1, col, grid);
    dfs(row, col - 1, grid);
    dfs(row, col + 1, grid);
}
```

## Mental Model

```text
Each cell = Node
Valid movement = Edge
```

## Problems

- 733. Flood Fill
- 200. Number of Islands
- 695. Max Area of Island

---

# 5. Component Size / Maximum Area

## Recognition

Look for:

- Size of a connected component
- Maximum island area
- Largest connected region

## Pattern

```java
private int dfs(int row, int col, int[][] grid) {
    if (
        row < 0 ||
        col < 0 ||
        row >= grid.length ||
        col >= grid[0].length ||
        grid[row][col] == 0
    ) {
        return 0;
    }

    grid[row][col] = 0;

    return 1
        + dfs(row - 1, col, grid)
        + dfs(row + 1, col, grid)
        + dfs(row, col - 1, grid)
        + dfs(row, col + 1, grid);
}
```

## Important Point

```java
return 1 + ...
```

The `1` counts the current cell.

## Problem

- 695. Max Area of Island

---

# 6. Shortest Path in an Unweighted Graph

## Recognition

Look for:

- Minimum steps
- Minimum moves
- Shortest path
- Fewest transformations
- Nearest destination

## Algorithm

```text
Use BFS
```

## Pattern

```java
Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

queue.offer(start);
visited[start] = true;

int distance = 0;

while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int size = queue.size();

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        int node = queue.poll();

        if (node == destination) {
            return distance;
        }

        for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
            if (!visited[neighbor]) {
                visited[neighbor] = true;
                queue.offer(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }

    distance++;
}

return -1;
```

## Important Rule

```text
One complete BFS level = One unit of distance
```

## Problems

- 1091. Shortest Path in Binary Matrix
- 127. Word Ladder
- 752. Open the Lock

---

# 7. Multi-Source BFS

## Recognition

Look for:

- Multiple starting sources
- Infection or fire spreading
- Rotting happening simultaneously
- Distance from the nearest source
- Minimum time until everything changes

## Main Idea

Add all starting sources to the queue before beginning BFS.

```java
Queue<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
    for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
        if (grid[row][col] == SOURCE) {
            queue.offer(new int[]{row, col});
        }
    }
}
```

Then perform normal level-order BFS:

```java
int time = 0;

while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int size = queue.size();

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        int[] current = queue.poll();

        // Process valid neighbors.
    }

    time++;
}
```

## Mental Model

```text
Level 0 → All starting sources
Level 1 → Cells reached after one step
Level 2 → Cells reached after two steps
```

## Problems

- 994. Rotting Oranges
- 542. 01 Matrix

---

# 8. Cycle Detection

## Undirected Graph

Use a `parent`.

```java
private boolean hasCycle(
    int node,
    int parent,
    List<List<Integer>> graph,
    boolean[] visited
) {
    visited[node] = true;

    for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
        if (!visited[neighbor]) {
            if (hasCycle(neighbor, node, graph, visited)) {
                return true;
            }
        } else if (neighbor != parent) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```

```text
Visited neighbor + not the parent = Cycle
```

## Directed Graph

Use three states:

```text
0 → Unvisited
1 → Currently in the DFS path
2 → Completely processed
```

```java
private boolean hasCycle(
    int node,
    List<List<Integer>> graph,
    int[] state
) {
    if (state[node] == 1) {
        return true;
    }

    if (state[node] == 2) {
        return false;
    }

    state[node] = 1;

    for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
        if (hasCycle(neighbor, graph, state)) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    state[node] = 2;
    return false;
}
```

```text
Reach state 1 again → Cycle exists
Reach state 2       → Already processed safely
```

## Problems

- 207. Course Schedule
- 261. Graph Valid Tree

---

# 9. Topological Sort

## Recognition

Look for:

- Course prerequisites
- Task ordering
- Dependency ordering
- Find a valid execution order

## Kahn's BFS Pattern

```java
Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

for (int node = 0; node < n; node++) {
    if (indegree[node] == 0) {
        queue.offer(node);
    }
}

List<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>();

while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int node = queue.poll();
    order.add(node);

    for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
        indegree[neighbor]--;

        if (indegree[neighbor] == 0) {
            queue.offer(neighbor);
        }
    }
}
```

## Cycle Check

```java
if (order.size() != n) {
    // The directed graph contains a cycle.
}
```

## Problems

- 207. Course Schedule
- 210. Course Schedule II

---

# Pattern Selection Cheat Sheet

```text
Visit all reachable nodes      → DFS or BFS

Check if a path exists        → DFS or BFS

Count connected groups        → Outer loop + DFS/BFS

Find component size           → DFS returning int

Minimum steps or moves        → BFS

Multiple starting sources     → Multi-source BFS

Undirected cycle              → Parent tracking

Directed cycle                → Three-state DFS

Dependency ordering           → Topological sort
```

---

# DFS vs BFS

## Choose DFS

```text
Explore a complete component
Count islands or regions
Calculate component size
Detect cycles using recursion
```

## Choose BFS

```text
Minimum steps
Shortest unweighted path
Level-by-level traversal
Simultaneous spreading
Nearest source
```

---

# Complexity

## Adjacency List

```text
Time  → O(V + E)
Space → O(V)
```

## Grid

```text
Time  → O(rows × columns)
Space → O(rows × columns)
```

---

# Golden Rules

1. First identify the nodes and edges.
2. Determine whether the graph is directed or undirected.
3. Use `visited` to prevent repeated exploration.
4. Mark BFS nodes visited when adding them to the queue.
5. Use an outer loop for disconnected graphs.
6. Use BFS for shortest paths in unweighted graphs.
7. Use multi-source BFS when several sources act simultaneously.
8. Undirected cycles need parent tracking.
9. Directed cycles need current-path tracking.
10. A grid is often a graph hidden inside a matrix.

